XOP: VPPERM / VPERMIL2 select the wrong source, VPMACS(S)WD multiply the odd words, VPSHL/VPSHA counts wrap instead of saturating, VPROT/VPSHL/VPSHA VEX.W roles inverted, VPSHA decoded at 88-8B instead of 98-9B

Six independent defects in the XOP vector handlers and decoder, all against the AMD
definition (AMD64 APM Vol. 4 "128-Bit and 256-Bit XOP and FMA4 Instructions";
the Microsoft XOP intrinsic reference quotes the same semantics; LLVM's
X86ShuffleDecode / X86InstComments, written and tested against Bulldozer
hardware, agree on the two selects):

1. VPPERM: selector values 0..15 pick bytes of the FIRST source (src1, the
   VEX.vvvv register) and 16..31 bytes of the second — "the 32-byte
   concatenation src2:src1 with src1 low". The handler picked src1 when bit 4
   was SET. (LLVM: vpperm(a0, a1, <8,24,9,25,...>) is unpckhbw(a0, a1).)

2. VPERMIL2PS / VPERMIL2PD (xmm_permil2ps/pd): selector values 0..3 pick src1
   and 4..7 (bit 2 set) pick src2. The helper returned op1 (src1) for bit 2
   set. (LLVM: vpermil2ps(a0, a1, <1,1,7,4>) is a0[1],a0[1],a1[3],a1[0].)

3. VPMACSWD / VPMACSSWD: "each EVEN-indexed 16-bit signed integer in src1 is
   multiplied by the corresponding one in src2 ... the odd-indexed values are
   ignored" (r[i] = src1[2*i] * src2[2*i] + src3[i]). The handlers used words
   1, 3, 5, 7.

4. VPSHLB/W/D/Q and VPSHAB/W/D/Q (xmm_pshl*/xmm_psha*): "if a shift count is
   greater than 7 [15/31/63] the result is 0; if less than -7 [...] the result
   is 0 (logical) / -1 if the value is negative, 0 otherwise (arithmetic)".
   The helpers masked the magnitude with width-1, so a count of 8 on bytes
   left the element unshifted. xmm_pshlw additionally read its count through
   xmmubyte, so a negative word count could never shift right.

5. VPROT/VPSHL/VPSHA variable forms (XOP9 90-9B): the opmap binds VEX.W the
   wrong way round, exactly as 0012 found for the is4 rows. The AMD APM's
   W = 0 encoding is "xmm1, xmm2/m128, xmm3" — ModRM.rm is the DATA (src1,
   may be memory) and VEX.vvvv the count — and W = 1 is "xmm1, xmm2,
   xmm3/m128" (vvvv the data, rm the count). The opmap had W0 on the
   VdqHdqWdq rows (data from vvvv, count from rm), so every register form
   rotated/shifted the count vector by the data and every memory form took
   the memory operand in the wrong role.

6. VPSHAB/W/D/Q were decoded at XOP9 opcodes 88-8B. AMD's map 9 places them
   at 98-9B (VPROT 90-93, VPSHL 94-97, VPSHA 98-9B; LLVM's X86InstrXOP.td and
   binutils encode them there), so the architectural encodings raised #UD and
   four undefined opcodes executed instead.

Fixed in avx/xop.cc (VPPERM select, the four VPMACS(S)WD word indices),
simd_int.h (the permil2 selects, the eight shift helpers) and
decoder/fetchdecode_opmap_xop.cc (ATTR_VEX_W0/W1 exchanged on the 12 XOP9
90-9B groups; the four VPSHA groups moved from 88-8B to 98-9B, 88-8B are #UD
again). VPROT*'s count itself is unaffected: a rotate count IS taken modulo
the width.

Found 2026-09-14 by the hybrid-x86-oracle differential harness diffing the
zens model of the XOP vector group. Present on upstream master as of
2026-09-14. No XOP silicon was available; the AMD manual, the MS intrinsic
reference and LLVM's hardware-validated decoders are the authority.

